    "sha": "8dfc8e311b0efba69f6a4dd2b41c0189be81ddef",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOVmofv9oAKDhkZmM4ZTMxMWIwZWZiYTY5ZjZhNGRkMmI0MWMwMTg5YmU4MWRkZWY",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "bherbruck",
        "email": "brennen.herbruck@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-08T01:07:00Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "bherbruck",
        "email": "brennen.herbruck@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-08T01:07:00Z"
      },
      "message": "fix(motion): the axis fit reads flats hiding in a fine ball's latitudes\n\nA finely meshed UV ball puts every facet's normal line through its\ncentre, so a rod or flat bar through that centre shared its pencil, and\nthe ball's continuous latitudes swallowed the rod's faces into one wide\nclass the ring check skips: a trusted cross axis that would turn a\n100 mm rod into a disc. Wide classes are now cut into latitude bins whose\nbearings must not fold (1st-4th harmonic resultant); a pencil whose\nfolded area passes 2 % of the inliers does not bear the axis out.\n\nFixes #237\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
      "tree": {
